# Environment template — copy to `.env` and fill in. The app runs without any of
# these set (PageSpeed runs keyless at lower rate limits; crawl discovery works
# without search). `.env` is gitignored; this template is committed.

# --- PageSpeed Insights (the /pagespeed page) ------------------------------
# A Google API key is effectively REQUIRED: keyless PSI is now capped at a 0
# daily quota (instant HTTP 429). The key's GCP project must have the "PageSpeed
# Insights API" ENABLED. Free tier with a key: 25,000 requests/day, ~240/minute.
#   1. Console → enable the API: console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/pagespeedonline.googleapis.com
#   2. APIs & Services → Credentials → create/reuse an API key on that project.
# If PAGESPEED_API_KEY is unset, the engine falls back to GOOGLE_API_KEY below —
# so a single key works for both, as long as PSI is enabled on its project.
PAGESPEED_API_KEY=

# --- AI analysis: which AI runs it (optional) ------------------------------
# Score analysis runs on YOUR AI — LightAudit Score ships no AI credentials.
#
# Recipe — Claude as your web-research AI. Claude is the only provider that can
# research current fixes on the web and cite real sources, so this is the
# fully-featured setup. Three steps:
#
#   1. Select the Claude provider. It is the default, so the line is optional:
#        LH_ANALYSIS_PROVIDER=claude
#   2. Sign in. Nothing to add here if Claude Code is logged in on this machine
#      (your Claude Pro / Max subscription pays for the analysis). To bill an
#      Anthropic API account instead, paste a key from console.anthropic.com →
#      API keys. A key set here wins over the login:
#        ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#      Optionally pin the model (leave unset for the SDK's current default):
#        LH_ANALYSIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5
#   3. Give it the web: switch on CrawlForge in Settings → Web research, or
#      declare another research MCP server — both are explained in the next
#      section. Without a research server Claude still analyses, but from the
#      audit data alone, and the result is badged "no web research".
#
#   Restart the server after editing this file. Settings → AI analysis shows
#   which provider, model and research server are active, and can also switch
#   provider without a restart: Claude, any model a local Ollama has installed,
#   or the OpenAI-compatible endpoint configured below. That choice is saved in
#   the app's own data and overrides the two selection lines below —
#   LH_ANALYSIS_PROVIDER and LH_ANALYSIS_MODEL — until you clear it with
#   "Use .env instead". Endpoints and keys are only ever read from this file.
#
# Pick one provider:
#
#   claude             (default) your existing Claude Code / Max login on this
#                      machine, or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY if you set one. This is the
#                      only provider that can research fixes on the web and cite
#                      real sources (see the research MCP server below).
#   ollama             a model running locally under Ollama. Private and free,
#                      but it can't browse: analyses are diagnosed from the audit
#                      data alone and are badged "no web research" in the UI.
#   openai-compatible  any endpoint speaking OpenAI's chat-completions API —
#                      OpenAI, OpenRouter, LM Studio, vLLM. Also data-only.
# LH_ANALYSIS_PROVIDER=claude

# Claude only — leave unset to use the Claude Code / Max login on this machine.
# Set it to bill an Anthropic API account instead (console.anthropic.com → API
# keys). Read from the environment at request time; never stored by the app.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Model id for the selected provider. Leave unset for Claude to use its default
# (or pin one, e.g. claude-opus-5); required for ollama and openai-compatible.
# Bigger local models reason about a full audit noticeably better — prefer 14B
# and up.
# LH_ANALYSIS_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:14b

# Ollama only — where it listens (the /v1 suffix is added for you).
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:14b

# openai-compatible only — the endpoint and your own key. Read from the
# environment at request time; never stored by the app.
# LH_ANALYSIS_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com/v1
# LH_ANALYSIS_API_KEY=

# --- AI analysis: web research (optional) ----------------------------------
# AI analysis always diagnoses from the Lighthouse data. To make it also research
# current fixes on the web (so each recommendation cites a real source), give it a
# research MCP server — web search + page-reading tools the Claude provider can
# drive. Two ways:
#
#   CrawlForge (one switch, nothing to put in this file). Get a free key at
#   https://www.crawlforge.dev/signup, run `npx crawlforge-setup` once (it stores
#   the key in ~/.crawlforge/config.json, where the server reads it itself), then
#   enable it in Settings → Web research. LightAudit Score launches a pinned
#   `npx -y crawlforge-mcp-server@<version>` on demand and never reads, stores,
#   or forwards the key. Every call is metered on YOUR CrawlForge account.
#   To run a newer CrawlForge release than the pinned default (shown in Settings),
#   name it here — an exact version only; ranges and tags are ignored:
# LH_CRAWLFORGE_VERSION=5.6.0
#
#   Any other MCP server (advanced). Declare it as `mcpServers.research` in an MCP
#   config file. By default LightAudit Score reads .mcp.json from its working directory;
#   these override that. When CrawlForge is switched on it takes precedence.
#   Note: an `env` block you declare there is passed to the `claude` CLI on its
#   command line, so prefer servers that read their own credentials.
# LH_RESEARCH_MCP_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/mcp.json
# LH_RESEARCH_MCP_SERVER=research

# --- Crawl discovery / web search (optional) -------------------------------
# Used by the "Crawl a site" URL discovery. Separate from PAGESPEED_API_KEY.
SEARCH_PROVIDER=google
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_SEARCH_ENGINE_ID=

# --- Auditing a protected site (optional) ----------------------------------
# Credentials for the site you AUDIT — a staging environment or a logged-in
# page. These are the site's secrets, not LightAudit Score's: they are read here,
# inside the audit worker and the crawler, and are never written to the
# database, to a report file, or to a log. A run's history record keeps only the
# header/cookie NAMES, with every value stored as "[redacted]".
#
# The same three mechanisms are also available per-batch in the New Audit form's
# Authentication panel (held in memory for that batch only). Put a value here
# instead when you reuse it; a per-batch value overrides this file entry by
# entry. PageSpeed Insights runs on Google's servers and cannot reach a page
# only your machine can, so it never uses these — the /pagespeed form does not
# offer authentication and the API rejects the combination.
#
# REQUIRED for any of the three below to be sent at all: the hosts they belong
# to, comma-separated. Without this they are ignored, because a value in this
# file is ambient — it would otherwise ride along on every audit you ever run,
# so a batch of competitor URLs would each receive your staging password. An
# entry may carry a port (box.local:8443) and may start with `*.` to cover
# subdomains only (*.preview.example.com matches pr-42.preview.example.com, but
# not the apex).
# LH_AUDIT_CREDENTIAL_HOSTS=staging.example.com,*.preview.example.com
#
# Note on scope: Chrome applies these headers per PAGE, not per origin, so they
# ride every request the audited page makes — including to third parties it
# loads. Prefer a credential scoped to the site under audit (a staging or
# preview token) over a production session cookie.
#
# HTTP basic auth as `user:password` (the password may contain colons; only the
# first colon separates the two):
# LH_AUDIT_BASIC_AUTH=
# Extra request headers as a JSON object — a preview/bypass token, a bearer:
# LH_AUDIT_EXTRA_HEADERS={"X-Preview-Token":"…"}
# Cookies, either as the Cookie header you copy out of DevTools
# (`session=…; csrf=…`) or as a JSON object:
# LH_AUDIT_COOKIES=

# --- Storage paths (optional) ----------------------------------------------
# Override where the SQLite DB and generated reports live (default ./data).
# LH_DATA_DIR=./data
# LH_DB_PATH=./data/lighthouse.db

# --- Local server (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# `npm start` binds 127.0.0.1 and gates every route behind a per-install session
# token: it prints (and opens) a http://127.0.0.1:3000/?token=… link, and opening
# it once sets the session cookie. The token is generated on first start and
# kept in <LH_DATA_DIR>/session-token. Pin your own instead (32+ characters):
# LH_SESSION_TOKEN=
# Don't open the link in a browser on start. Recommended on a shared machine:
# while a browser is being launched, the link (token included) is visible in
# the process list to other local users.
# LH_OPEN_BROWSER=0
# Note: browsers scope cookies by host, not port, so any other server you run on
# 127.0.0.1 also receives the session cookie. State-changing requests are
# refused unless they come from LightAudit Score's own origin, which limits the blast
# radius — but don't run software you distrust on loopback.
# The server only answers requests whose Host header is a loopback name
# (127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]) — the defence against DNS rebinding. If you
# deliberately bind elsewhere (HOST=0.0.0.0), list the hostnames you will use:
# LH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=192.168.1.20,mybox.lan
# PORT=3000
# HOST=127.0.0.1
